e": 21.731295, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-1-4842-9705-6_2"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2023]]}, "ISBN": ["9781484297056"], "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4842-9705-6_2", "published": {"date-parts": [[2023]]}, "assertion": [{"value": "2 August 2023", "order": 1, "name": "first_online", "label": "First Online", "group": {"name": "ChapterHistory", "label": "Chapter History"}}]}, {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2025, 3, 28]], "date-time": "2025-03-28T10:27:10Z", "timestamp": 1743157630921, "version": "3.40.3"}, "publisher-location": "Berkeley, CA", "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "Apress", "isbn-type": [{"type": "electronic", "value": "9781484297056"}], "license": [{"start": {"date-parts": [[2023, 1, 1]], "date-time": "2023-01-01T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1672531200000}, "content-version": "tdm", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "https://www.springernature.com/gp/researchers/text-and-data-mining"}, {"start": {"date-parts": [[2023, 1, 1]], "date-time": "2023-01-01T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1672531200000}, "content-version": "vor", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "https://www.springernature.com/gp/researchers/text-and-data-mining"}], "content-domain": {"domain": ["link.springer.com"], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "published-print": {"date-parts": [[2023]]}, "DOI": "10.1007/978-1-4842-9705-6_5", "type": "book-chapter", "created": {"date-parts": [[2023, 8, 1]], "date-time": "2023-08-01T15:02:01Z", "timestamp": 1690902121000}, "update-policy": "https://doi.org/10.1007/springer_crossmark_policy", "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["Re-execute SAP WORKFLOW Agent rules(SWI1_RULE)"], "prefix": "10.1007", "author": [{"given": "Arghadip", "family": "Kar", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"role": "author", "vocabulary": "crossref"}]}], "member": "297", "published-online": {"date-parts": [[2023, 8, 2]]}, "container-title": ["SAP Workflow Administration and Monitoring"], "language": "en", "link": [{"URL": "https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-1-4842-9705-6_5", "content-type": "unspecified", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "similarity-checking"}], "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2023, 8, 1]], "date-time": "2023-08-01T15:05:58Z", "timestamp": 1690902358000}, "score": 21.638924, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-1-4842-9705-6_5"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2023]]}, "ISBN": ["9781484297056"], "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4842-9705-6_5", "published": {"date-parts": [[2023]]}, "assertion": [{"value": "2 August 2023", "order": 1, "name": "first_online", "label": "First Online", "group": {"name": "ChapterHistory", "label": "Chapter History"}}]}, {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 6, 23]], "date-time": "2026-06-23T09:53:19Z", "timestamp": 1782208399056, "version": "3.54.5"}, "posted": {"date-parts": [[2026, 6, 23]]}, "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "American Chemical Society (ACS)", "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "abstract": "<jats:p>Molecular dynamics (MD) simulation links atomic structure to thermodynamic, transport, and interfacial properties, but translating a scientific question into an appropriate GROMACS workflow remains a practical barrier. A complete GROMACS workflow spans structure, topology, MDP, TPR, trajectory, energy, index, and output files, and many errors are scientific rather than syntactic, surviving execution while quietly invalidating the result. Prior MD agents have focused on single-script generation, mainly for LAMMPS, which does not capture this multi-file, multi-stage structure. We present GROMACS-Agent, a multi-agent prototype that treats GROMACS automation as text-to-workflow generation rather than text-to-code generation. The system combines a Manager Agent, Planner Agent, Retriever, Gromacs Worker Agent, Gromacs Evaluator Agent, and Code Executor Agent, supported by two curated datasets: GSCF for workflow generation and GEQS for workflow evaluation. For each task, the agent returns required files, a command sequence, expected outputs, validation notes, an evaluator score, and correction suggestions, with selected safe-execution checks for allow-listed commands. In a five-task pilot comparison, GROMACS-Agent improved evaluator-assessed correctness from 6.6/10 for baseline LLM responses to 10/10, and a single-user timing pilot showed a substantial reduction in local workflow-preparation time. These early results position GROMACS-Agent as a practical, human-in-the-loop step toward reliable GROMACS workflow automation, with expert review and physical validation still required before execution.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.26434/chemrxiv.15005078/v1", "type": "posted-content", "created": {"date-parts": [[2026, 6, 23]], "date-time": "2026-06-23T09:38:02Z", "timestamp": 1782207482000}, "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["GROMACS-Agent: Text-to-Workflow Generation for Molecular Dynamics A multi-agent framework for topology-aware GROMACS workflow generation, evaluation, correction, and selected safe execution"], "prefix": "10.26434", "author": [{"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0009-0001-6188-0860", "authenticated-orcid": false, "given": "Muhammad Sohail", "family": "Ahmad", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [{"name": "School of Energy and Environment", "place": ["Nanjing, China"]}, {"name": "Southeast University", "place": ["Nanjing, China"]}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "member": "316", "link": [{"URL": "https://chemrxiv.org/doi/pdf/10.26434/chemrxiv.15005078/v1", "content-type": "unspecified", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "similarity-checking"}], "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2026, 6, 23]], "date-time": "2026-06-23T09:38:02Z", "timestamp": 1782207482000}, "score": 21.212215, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://chemrxiv.org/doi/full/10.26434/chemrxiv.15005078/v1"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2026, 6, 23]]}, "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.26434/chemrxiv.15005078/v1", "published": {"date-parts": [[2026, 6, 23]]}, "subtype": "preprint"}, {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2024, 5, 1]], "date-time": "2024-05-01T17:03:18Z", "timestamp": 1714582998241}, "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "InTech", "isbn-type": [{"value": "9789533071763", "type": "print"}], "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "DOI": "10.5772/15903", "type": "book-chapter", "created": {"date-parts": [[2012, 3, 29]], "date-time": "2012-03-29T04:25:51Z", "timestamp": 1332995151000}, "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["Multi-Agent Models in Workflow Design"], "prefix": "10.5772", "author": [{"given": "Victoria", "family": "Iordan", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"role": "author", "vocabulary": "crossref"}]}], "member": "3774", "published-online": {"date-parts": [[2011, 4, 1]]}, "container-title": ["Multi-Agent Systems - Modeling, Interactions, Simulations and Case Studies"], "language": "en", "link": [{"URL": "http://www.intechopen.com/download/pdf/14489", "content-type": "unspecified", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "similarity-checking"}], "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2016, 12, 29]], "date-time": "2016-12-29T05:39:46Z", "timestamp": 1482989986000}, "score": 20.385365, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "http://www.intechopen.com/books/multi-agent-systems-modeling-interactions-simulations-and-case-studies/multi-agent-models-in-workflow-design"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2011, 4, 1]]}, "ISBN": ["9789533071763"], "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.5772/15903", "published": {"date-parts": [[2011, 4, 1]]}}, {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 6, 17]], "date-time": "2026-06-17T21:51:46Z", "timestamp": 1781733106325, "version": "3.54.5"}, "posted": {"date-parts": [[2026, 1, 29]]}, "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "Wiley", "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "abstract": "<jats:p>Geophysical methods provide powerful tools for imaging the subsurface,\nbut their complex workflows present a significant barrier to\nnon-specialists. While a growing ecosystem of open-source Python\npackages exists, their steep, individual learning curves and the need to\nchain multiple tools for a comprehensive investigation create challenges\neven for experts. We introduce a Generalizable Automated Geophysical\nAgent Workflow (GAGAW), an LLM-based framework designed to automate\nthese complex tasks by managing various open-source tools through a\nnatural language interface. We demonstrate GAGAW in hydrogeophysics to\nmap subsurface hydrology, using three workflows widely adopted across\ngeophysics: time\u2011lapse inversion, multi\u2011source data fusion, and\npetrophysical conversion. In three examples, simple natural language\nprompts produced reproducible resistivity models, water\u2011content\nestimates, climate analyses, and comprehensive reports. I also outline\ncurrent limitations and future work on geophysical agents and show that\nGAGAW establishes a practical pattern for accessible, cross\u2011modal\ngeophysical analysis for a broader community of domain experts.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.22541/essoar.176336946.65126612/v2", "type": "posted-content", "created": {"date-parts": [[2026, 1, 29]], "date-time": "2026-01-29T09:16:56Z", "timestamp": 1769678216000}, "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["A Generalizable Automated Geophysical Agent Workflow in Subsurface Hydrology"], "prefix": "10.22541", "author": [{"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8979-182X", "authenticated-orcid": false, "given": "Hang", "family": "Chen", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [{"name": "The University of Iowa"}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}], "member": "311", "link": [{"URL": "https://essopenarchive.org/doi/pdf/10.22541/essoar.176336946.65126612/v2", "content-type": "unspecified", "c